            <ArticleTitle>Dobutamine stress MRI for the assessment of coronary artery disease: initial clinical experience in Israel.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>205-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Over the past few years dobutamine stress magnetic resonance (DSMR) has proven its efficacy as an integral part of the diagnosis of coronary artery disease (CAD).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To present the feasibility and safety of DSMR in Israel.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Thirty patients with suspected or known CAD were studied. DSMR images were acquired during short breath-holds in three short axis views and four-, two-, and three-chamber views. Patients were examined at rest and during a standard dobutamine-atropine protocol. Regional wall motion was assessed in a 16-segment model and the image quality was evaluated using a four-point scale for the visibility of the endocardial border.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">In 28 patients (93.4%) DSMR was successfully performed and completed within an average of 55 +/- 6 minutes. One patient could not be examined because of claustrophobia and another patient, who was on beta-blockers, did not reach the target heart rate. Image quality was excellent and there was no difference between the rest and stress images in short axis (3.91 +/- 0.29 vs. 3.88 +/- 0.34, P = 0.13, respectively) and long axis (3.83 +/- 0.38 vs. 3.70 +/- 0.49, P = 0.09, respectively) views. Segmental intra-observer agreement for wall motion contractility at rest and stress cine images was almost perfect (K = 0.88, 95% confidence interval = 0.93-0.84, and K = 0.82, 95% CI = 0.88-0.76) respectively. No serious side effects were observed during DSMR.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The present study confirms the feasibility, safety and excellent image quality of DSMR for the diagnosis of coronary artery diseases.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
